  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Warmer and breezy today with scattered showers \nthrough Thursday. \n\nSurface high pressure centered over the Mid-Atlantic region and \nridging northward across the eastern Great Lakes early this morning \nwill gradually give way to a surface low and associated fronts \nthroughout the remainder of the day. However, before getting to far \nahead of ourselves, with the ridge of high pressure currently \noverhead, another cool night is expected with lows dropping down \ninto the low to mid 40s, and a range of 30s across the Tug Hill \nregion and into Lewis county. This being said, patchy to areas of \nfrost are possible across the North County.\n\nNow heading into the remainder of today, a tightening pressure \ngradient between the approaching cold front and exiting surface low \nalong with warm air advection beneath a 40 knot low level jet will \noverspread the area. As such a southwest wind to around 3kft during \npeak heating will support breezy conditions across the region, \nespecially for areas northeast of Lake Erie. Wind gusts will range \nbetween 35 and 40 mph across the Niagara Frontier this afternoon.\n\nAdditionally, increasing moisture ahead of the associated shortwave \ntrough and cold front will support widely scattered showers across \nthe region this afternoon/evening. In the wake of the frontal \npassage tonight not only will winds diminish, but showers will \nremain likely. Rainfall amounts will be widely scattered and be less \nthan 0.15 inches.\n\nLooking into later half of the work week, a longwave trough will \nmove into the Great Lakes region, supporting seasonal temperatures \nwith highs in the low to mid 60s. A cool, westerly flow across the \nLakes will support lake effect and diurnally driven showers, with \nmuch of the focus across north-central New York Thursday.",
